{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792161716,"nonce":"NDLCA70dCvwRRhY19LZzy5jTFDY=","title":"Generic native-call dispatch heap-allocates its argument words every call","message":"crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/natives.rs:371, the integer/pointer\nnative-call path builds `let mut words: Vec\u003ci64\u003e = Vec::with_capacity(args.len() + 1)`\nand fills it via push/extend, for every native call with \u003c= 12 words\n(the function rejects more at line ~375: \"Native call with {} args not\nyet supported\"). Since the max is already known and bounded at compile\ntime, this is a heap allocation on a path that could use a fixed stack\narray instead.\n\ndispatch_float_native, a few hundred lines below in the same file,\nalready avoids this: it uses `let mut kinds = [0u8; MAX_ARGS]` (a stack\narray) rather than a Vec for its own per-call scratch state. The integer\npath is the odd one out.\n\nNative calls are extremely frequent in an ashui/blinc_abi-backed app\n(every Signal.get(), every native property write, every CSS native\ncall), so this is on a hot path: a small (\u003c=104-byte) malloc/free pair\nper native call, system-level churn that would not show in Haxe's own\nhl.Gc.stats() but would show as allocator/GC-adjacent CPU cost.\n\nSuggested fix: replace the Vec with a fixed `[i64; 13]` (context + up to\n12 args) and a length counter, matching dispatch_float_native's existing\npattern, since the bound is already enforced.\n\nSurfaced while investigating ashui's CodeWorkbench demo memory and CPU\n(ashui git-bug e1a026f, 0973e0c). Not yet measured in isolation; a\nmicrobenchmark before/after would confirm the win's size.","files":null}]}